## Whole-run definition of done

- `@naxodev/music-core` ships a built Node-compatible daemon and an Effect-v4-backed client with a schema-validated, versioned, bounded Unix-socket protocol.
- The packed daemon is proven under Node from an isolated tarball install by a real handshake/replay and clean idle shutdown, not merely by a Bun `--help` invocation.
- Concurrent first use creates one same-user daemon; stale artifacts and shutdown are safe; zero clients eventually release the provider and process.
- Supported old/current package builds negotiate against and share the live daemon. An unsupported client fails actionably without unlinking the live socket, killing healthy sessions, spawning a competing daemon, or retry-looping.
- A tested 24-client scenario proves one provider/stream/poll owner, immediate latest-state replay, healthy fan-out under a slow client, and one global command FIFO.
- Reconnect starts or finds one replacement daemon after genuine loss, rejects old-generation events, and never silently replays an indeterminate command.
- OpenCode and Pi contain no per-instance provider creation, provider stream restart, fallback polling, playback clock, or general transport queue. They dispose only their client/presentation resources.
- Existing 3/5/8 poll, 1/2/4/8 stream restart, stale-result rejection, nowplaying fallback, controls, errors, waveform, artwork identity/cache, UI, reload, and shutdown behavior is retained at its new owner.
- Native media-provider artwork reads are centralized/deduplicated; OpenCode-specific catalog resolution/rendering and all UI state remain client-local.
- Package tarballs contain every runtime source/binary needed by isolated OpenCode and Pi installs, and package smokes use packed artifacts rather than workspace-only resolution.
- `docs/music-session-architecture.html` is preserved as intentional worktree content and accurately presents the selected daemon topology as current.
